 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "moed_katan.8a.17",
   "daf": "8a",
   "sentenceId": 16,
   "he": "וְלֹא יְעַרְעֵר עַל מֵתוֹ. מַאי לֹא יְעַרְעֵר עַל מֵתוֹ? אָמַר רַב: כַּד הָדַר סַפְדָנָא בְּמַעְרְבָא, אָמְרִי: יִבְכּוֹן עִמֵּיהּ כָּל מְרִירֵי לִיבָּא.",
   "en": "§ It is taught in the mishna: «One may not arouse» lamentation «for his deceased» relative during the thirty days before a Festival. The Gemara asks: «What is» meant by: «One may not arouse» lamentation «for his deceased» relative? «Rav said: In the West,» Eretz Yisrael, «whenever a» professional «eulogizer would circulate» and ply his trade, «they would say: Let all those of bitter heart weep with him.» When they would say this, all those who had recently suffered losses would recall their pain and lament their losses."
  }
